WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.640
There are times when the data isn't available and&nbsp;
you need to collect your own. Now, when talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.040
about the various methods that can be used,&nbsp;
there are a few factors that distinguish them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.920
For instance, there's the level of intervention.&nbsp;
Is it observational, which is strictly hands off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.520
or is it experimental, where you get in the mix&nbsp;
and you create a situation? What kind of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.920
type do you have? Is it quantitative, or numerical&nbsp;
data, which is what we often use in data science?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.480
Or, is it qualitative, specifically text, which&nbsp;
can be analyzed but requires different approaches?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:46.000
And how about the location? Is it online or&nbsp;
is it in person? All of these combinations can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.520
be used to gather data that you need,&nbsp;
if you're careful about your design.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.840
For instance, let's look at interviews.&nbsp;
These are ideal for new topics or audiences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.880
They can be structured or unstructured. However,&nbsp;
interviews are generally very time-consuming, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.560
they may require a very specialized training. This&nbsp;
is one of the situations where you actually may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.360
want to hire an anthropologist or a sociologist&nbsp;
to come in and conduct the research for you.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:21.040
Next, surveys. They can be open-ended or&nbsp;
closed-ended. They're very easy to administer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.960
online to a lot of people. But, you need to watch&nbsp;
out for wording and sampling bias in order to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.520
the information that's gonna be really most useful&nbsp;
in helping you reach your goals in your project.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:38.160
Next is card sorting. This is where participants&nbsp;
sort topics that are written on different cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.640
Card sorting is useful for determining&nbsp;
categories and hierarchies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.640
It's also a great way to check&nbsp;
the usability of a web site.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.920
You can actually do different kinds of card&nbsp;
sorting in terms of an exploratory one to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.400
find out how you should do it and another kind&nbsp;
to see how well your proposed solution works.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.840
Next, how about experiments? This&nbsp;
is where you have a manipulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.160
That's where you get involved, and you&nbsp;
cause a situation to be different for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.440
one group of people or another. Some people&nbsp;
freak out about the word "manipulation",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:17.280
and think it's about coercion or mind control. It&nbsp;
simply means about creating different conditions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.840
And it is generally considered the best&nbsp;
method for cause-and-effect inferences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.760
On the other hand, in most situations,&nbsp;
experimentation can be labor-intensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:33.520
and can require very extensive specialized&nbsp;
training. There is one major exception to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.400
and that's A/B testing, which is used on web&nbsp;
pages where you have two versions of a web page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.520
or an element on a web page, and it's randomly&nbsp;
assigned, one version or another, to viewers.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.840
In summary, to start a data science project,&nbsp;
you’ll first get exactly the data that you need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.040
If what it doesn't exist, you will have&nbsp;
to collect the new data. Many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.120
methods may require in-depth training, and&nbsp;
you may want to hire consultants for them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.840
Also, there are important ethical&nbsp;
issues when conducting research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:12.960
especially when it's live and in person.&nbsp;
You'll want to consult an expert about those.

